                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="stats-box">
                    <h4>End Point (Latest)</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>${last_close:.2f}</strong> on {end_date.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')}</li>
                        <li>{((last_close/first_close)-1)*100:.1f}% total return</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight">
                <strong>Analyst Note:</strong> Gold has experienced multiple distinct regimes over 25 years: a secular bull market from 2000-2011, a correction and consolidation phase 2012-2018, and renewed strength post-2019 reaching new all-time highs. The 2008 financial crisis created a brief but sharp spike in both prices and volatility.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 2: Volatility Analysis -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>2. Volatility Analysis & Regime Detection</h2>

            <div class="regime-legend">
                <div class="regime-item"><div class="regime-dot" style="background: var(--green);"></div> Low Volatility</div>
                <div class="regime-item"><div class="regime-dot" style="background: var(--blue);"></div> Medium Volatility</div>
                <div class="regime-item"><div class="regime-dot" style="background: var(--red);"></div> High Volatility</div>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                {vol_timeseries_svg}
                <p class="chart-caption">Figure 2: Rolling volatility time series (20-day and 60-day) with overall average reference line.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                {vol_chart_svg}
                <p class="chart-caption">Figure 3: Volatility regime classification based on 60-day rolling volatility percentiles.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Volatility Regime Statistics</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Regime</th>
                    <th>Threshold</th>
                    <th>Days in Regime</th>
                    <th>% of Period</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="color: var(--green);">Low Volatility</td>
                    <td>&lt; {low_vol_threshold*100:.1f}%</td>
                    <td>{(df['VolRegime']=='low').sum():,}</td>
                    <td>{(df['VolRegime']=='low').sum()/len(df)*100:.1f}%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="color: var(--blue);">Medium Volatility</td>
                    <td>{low_vol_threshold*100:.1f}% – {high_vol_threshold*100:.1f}%</td>
                    <td>{(df['VolRegime']=='medium').sum():,}</td>
                    <td>{(df['VolRegime']=='medium').sum()/len(df)*100:.1f}%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="color: var(--red);">High Volatility</td>
                    <td>&gt; {high_vol_threshold*100:.1f}%</td>
                    <td>{(df['VolRegime']=='high').sum():,}</td>
                    <td>{(df['VolRegime']=='high').sum()/len(df)*100:.1f}%</td>
                </tr>
            </table>

            <h3>Crisis Period Identification</h3>
            <p>Crisis periods are defined as days when 60-day annualized volatility exceeds the 90th percentile ({crisis_threshold*100:.1f}%).</p>
            <div class="highlight">
                <strong>Crisis Days Identified:</strong> {len(crisis_periods)} days across the dataset. These periods typically coincide with:
                <ul style="margin-top: 10px;">
                    <li>2008-2009 Global Financial Crisis</li>
                    <li>2011 US credit rating downgrade</li>
                    <li>2020 COVID-19 pandemic volatility</li>
                    <li>2022 rate hike shock environment</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 3: Seasonal Patterns -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>3. Seasonal & Calendar-Based Patterns</h2>

            <div class="chart-container">
                {seasonal_chart_svg}
                <p class="chart-caption">Figure 4: Average daily returns by month. Green bars indicate positive months, red indicates negative.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                {monthly_heatmap_svg}
                <p class="chart-caption">Figure 5: Monthly returns heatmap showing performance across years and months.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Monthly Return Statistics</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Month</th>
                    <th>Avg Daily Return</th>
                    <th>Std Dev</th>
                    <th>Annualized</th>
                    <th>t-Statistic</th>
                    <th>Significant?</th>
                </tr>
"""

# Add monthly data rows
month_names_list = ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar', 'Apr', 'May', 'Jun', 'Jul', 'Aug', 'Sep', 'Oct', 'Nov', 'Dec']
for m in range(1, 13):
    row = df[df['Month'] == m]['LogReturn']
    mean_ret = row.mean() * 100
    std_ret = row.std() * 100
    annualized = mean_ret * 12
    t_stat = row.mean() / (row.std() / np.sqrt(len(row)))
    p_val = 2 * (1 - abs(stats.t.cdf(t_stat, len(row)-1))) if len(row) > 1 else 1
    sig = "Yes" if p_val < 0.05 else "No"
    html_content += f"""                <tr>
                    <td>{month_names_list[m-1]}</td>
                    <td>{mean_ret:.4f}%</td>
                    <td>{std_ret:.4f}%</td>
                    <td>{annualized:.2f}%</td>
                    <td>{t_stat:.2f}</td>
                    <td style="color: {'green' if sig == 'Yes' else 'gray'}">{sig}</td>
                </tr>
"""

html_content += f"""            </table>

            <div class="methodology">
                <h4>Statistical Methodology</h4>
                <p>Seasonal significance is assessed using a two-tailed t-test on daily log returns. Returns with p-value &lt; 0.05 are considered statistically significant. Note that monthly sample sizes vary and early data (pre-2003) may have unreliable volume.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Day-of-Week Analysis</h3>
            <div class="stats-grid">
"""

dow_names = ['Monday', 'Tuesday', 'Wednesday', 'Thursday', 'Friday', 'Saturday', 'Sunday']
for dow in range(5):  # Trading days only
    row = df[df['DayOfWeek'] == dow]['LogReturn']
    mean_ret = row.mean() * 100
    html_content += f"""                <div class="stats-box">
                    <h4>{dow_names[dow]}</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Mean Return: <strong>{mean_ret:.4f}%</strong></li>
                        <li>Observations: {len(row):,}</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
"""

html_content += f"""            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 4: Return Distribution -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>4. Return Distribution & Tail Risk</h2>

            <div class="chart-container">
                {distribution_chart_svg}
                <p class="chart-caption">Figure 6: Histogram of daily returns with normal distribution overlay (left) and Q-Q plot (right). Deviation from the diagonal in the Q-Q plot indicates fat tails.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Distribution Characteristics</h3>
            <div class="stats-grid">
                <div class="stats-box">
                    <h4>Central Tendency</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Mean Daily Return: <strong>{returns_clean.mean()*100:.4f}%</strong></li>
                        <li>Median Return: <strong>{returns_clean.median()*100:.4f}%</strong></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="stats-box">
                    <h4>Dispersion</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Std Dev (daily): <strong>{returns_clean.std()*100:.4f}%</strong></li>
                        <li>Range: {returns_clean.min()*100:.2f}% to {returns_clean.max()*100:.2f}%</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="stats-box">
                    <h4>Shape Statistics</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Skewness: <strong>{skewness:.4f}</strong> ({'negative (left tail)' if skewness < 0 else 'positive (right tail)'})</li>
                        <li>Excess Kurtosis: <strong>{kurtosis:.4f}</strong> ({'leptokurtic (fat tails)' if kurtosis > 0 else 'platykurtic (thin tails)'})</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Percentile Distribution</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Percentile</th>
"""

for p in percentiles:
    html_content += f"""                    <td>{p}th</td>
"""

html_content += f"""                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Return (%)</th>
"""

for p in percentiles:
    html_content += f"""                    <td>{return_percentiles[percentiles.index(p)]*100:.2f}%</td>
"""

html_content += f"""                </tr>
            </table>

            <h3>Value-at-Risk (VaR) & Expected Shortfall</h3>
            <div class="stats-grid">
                <div class="stats-box">
                    <h4>95% VaR (1-day)</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>{var_95*100:.2f}%</strong></li>
                        <li>5% probability of losing more than this in a day</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="stats-box">
                    <h4>95% CVaR / Expected Shortfall</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>{cvar_95*100:.2f}%</strong></li>
                        <li>Average loss when VaR is breached</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="stats-box">
                    <h4>1% VaR (Tail Risk)</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>{np.percentile(returns_clean, 1)*100:.2f}%</strong></li>
                        <li>Extreme downside scenario</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight">
                <strong>Tail Risk Assessment:</strong> The excess kurtosis of {kurtosis:.2f} indicates that gold returns have fatter tails than a normal distribution. This means extreme moves (both up and down) occur more frequently than would be expected under Gaussian assumptions. The negative skew ({skewness:.2f}) indicates that large negative returns are more extreme than large positive ones.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 5: Momentum & Liquidity -->
        <div class="section">
